WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.300
What's up guys, my name is Colin McNeil for
theScore esports.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.900
A great man once posed the question, what's
beef?

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:16.660
Well to answer it, beef is when two people
or organizations have an on-going, mutually

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.060
disrespectful relationship.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.679
So for this Top 10, we scoured the esports
landscape.

00:00:22.679 --> 00:00:28.710
From Halo to League of Legends to find you
the spiciest rivalries we could.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.160
So without further ado let's get on with
it.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.340
Here are the Top 10 esports beefs.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:42.760
Kicking off our list at number 10 is TSM Reginald
versus CLG’s Hotshot GG

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:49.810
The TSM and CLG owners have fostered one of
esports' premiere rivalries since 2010, when

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.480
Reginald played 
for CLG.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.740
"I used to play with Regi, I used to be on a team with him.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.960
And,  it was for a short time, a very very short time."

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.820
Their individual rivalry quickly moved on to
the rift.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:09.480
(Casting)

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.020
After they became full time owners, the beef
went public.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.640
The rivalry would often include humiliating
bets.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.520
The beef evolved to the point where playing
for either team meant beefing with the other.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.880
"Against CLG my sodium levels kinda go out of control."

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.440
"They just have TSM's number."

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.400
"They're just FreeSM, you know?"

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.880
"As far as I know, the only NA team I'm worried about is Cloud 9. The rest are non-existent. Especially CLG."

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:48.680
To date TSM has won four more NA LCS Splits,
but CLG routinely posts strong results.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:57.080
CLG even beat TSM at Madison Square Gardens
in 2015 to claim an NA LCS Championship of

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:57.880
their own.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:04.039
A beef that started between two former players
has had a rippling effect on the two teams,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.560
a rivalry that is still felt today.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.720
"Hello and welcome to the Shanghai Major, presented by Perfect World. "

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:21.760
Dota 2’s Shanghai Major was problematic
for many reasons, but one controversy stands

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:28.280
above the rest: the firing of James “2GD”
Harding, which comes it at number nine on

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.000
our list.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.440
"So last night, I was perusing the hotel entertainment.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:42.160
It turned out, censorship, the Chinese hotel had disabled pornography.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.760
Don't get me wrong I watched it.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.760
Mr. Wang's amazing wheelchaired antics were pretty amazing."

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.760
"You can f--k off, Winter."

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.800
For his controversial commentary, Valve CEO
Gabe Newell made headlines by publicly denouncing

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.920
and firing Harding, calling him an ass in
a Reddit post.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.340
"Two things. James. We've had issues with James at previous events.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.620
Some Valve people lobbied to bring him back for Shanghai, feeling that he deserved another chance.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.200
That was a mistake. James is an ass, and we won't be working with him again."

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.700
Harding tried to clear his name and posted
screenshots of a conversation with Dota 2

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.980
developer IceFrog telling him to be himself.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:31.230
These days the infamous caster has retired
from the game, and is playing professional

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.520
Quake Champions.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:38.160
Coming in at number eight is Snip3down versus
Lethul.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.720
Yes, we are talking about a Halo beef.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:48.800
One week before the 2016 X-Games in Aspen,
Lethul left Evil Geniuses and joined rival

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.320
team CLG.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.300
"If there is one player on EG that you feel like you need to shut down

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.220
who is that player?"

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.240
"Probably Snip3down, he's like the best player, he's just so sick.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:06.080
You know, I get really worried when I see him and it's just the stuff he does is insane."

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.200
"Alright well any words here Snip3down?"

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.160
"I remember what happened the last time that exact quote was said to me

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:12.760
so watch your face."

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.200
That led EG team captain Snip3down to post
a tell-all YouTube video that explained the

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.280
situation.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:23.000
"I thought T.J. and I were friends.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:28.560
I feel like it was pretty out of the blue move.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.160
He didn't really communicate well with us at all with this.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.820
Best of luck to him in whatever he decides to choose

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.540
I really hope that it comes to bite him in the ass."

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:40.000
The antics continued when the X-Games rolled
around.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.960
EG would go on to win gold after a tight seven-game
series, and Snip3down did not let Lethul forget

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:47.440
it.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.520
"I told him before it started the last person that said that to me lost the series

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.440
and they lost the series too.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:54.240
So if you're going to talk trash you better back it up and we took that series home."

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.640
After the X-Games, CLG would win the HCS World
Championship.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:05.810
A roster shake-up would follow for the rest
of the league, and Snip3down left EG.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:12.320
Snip3down went on to join a dream team with
Envyus and the rivalry continued.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:16.520
Number seven goes to Doublelift and Aphromoo.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:21.050
The relationship between teammates is crucial
to craft a successful team.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:28.460
In late-2015, CLG’s League of Legends squad
underwent some massive changes as star AD

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:35.440
carry Doublelift left the team to join TSM,
leaving his long-time support Aphromoo.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.720
Doublelift and Aphromoo were friends, holding
down CLG’s bot lane for three years together.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.120
The duo tag-teamed many interviews together,
and were widely considered one of the best

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.560
bot lanes in LCS history, calling themselves
Rush Hour.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.720
Doublelift accused Aphromoo of forcing an
ultimatum on CLG.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.960
"The truth is Aphro went to the org and he said

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.520
it's either me or him referring to me, and I'm not playing with Peter next year."

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.160
The two players continued to make comments
back-and-forth on stream and in post game interviews.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.040
"The best decision moving forward, only because Peter

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.840
brings people down more often than not."

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.260
"Me and CLG had a falling out are you and Aphro still buddies?

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.040
No. Not really.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.200
But a falling out is one way to put it for sure."

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.400
Now, two have since made up, even bot laning
again together at the League of Legends All-Star

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.480
event in 2016.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:47.940
Reginald and Tryndamere didn't have the most
dramatic beef, but certainly one of the

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:53.060
most impactful, which is why it comes in at
number six on our list.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:07:01.260
During the 2016 NA LCS Summer Split, TSM owner
Reginald spoke out on how Riot’s major patches

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.600
affected the League of Legends competitive
scene.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.180
"It's really scary to own an esports team.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.400
Because the patches change a lot.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.960
And so when you own a team and the patches change in a really unfavorable way

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:18.880
for your players, then there is a higher chance that you'll do bad and you can get relegated.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:25.800
How the system works if you get relegated you're done. And because the Challenger

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.600
is so weak if you get relegated you pretty much lose everything."

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:31.200
How did Riot respond?

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.440
Well, Riot co-founder Marc ‘Tryndamere’
Merrill took to Reddit to explain, coining

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:40.600
the very meme-worthy phrase, “Love me some Regi."

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:46.520
"Love me some Regi, but if he’s so concerned about the financial health of his players

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:49.540
maybe he should spend some more of the millions he has made / makes

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:56.120
from League of Legends on paying them instead of investing in other eSports where he is losing money?

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:00.440
Regi is one of the generally “good guy” team owners

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:04.600
But when owners don’t want to shell out for top talent, that’s a tricky problem.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:08.320
We intentionally prioritized game health and viewer experience

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:13.680
Over the ability for teams / coaches to field 'safer' comps by lane swapping."

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:19.840
The back-and-forth sparked a bigger conversation
within the league, arguably paving the way

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.960
for NA LCS franchising.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:28.760
Coming in at number five, Sonic Fox vs Perfect Legend.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:36.240
Summer Jam 9 featured a Mortal Kombat X grudge
match between Sonic Fox and Perfect Legend.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.710
It started over a Twitter beef, and ended
with Sonic Fox mopping the floor with Perfect

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:45.120
Legend, beating him 10-0.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:51.640
Perfect Legend followed up his 10-0 loss by
downplaying Sonic Fox’s win, claiming the

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:57.880
original Twitter beef centred around
Fox’s Erron Black versus his Kung Lao.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:05.240
"What I did say, I would destroy his Erron Black, as you saw, that character was not on screen."

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:11.880
Tournament organizer Eric ‘Big E’ Small
quickly grabbed the mic from PL, and well,

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:14.360
you can see what happened next.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.800
"So do we have a three-to-five with the Erron Black?

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:22.880
three-out-of-five with the Erron Black? Three-out-of-five with the Erron Black?"

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:25.680
That’s right.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:29.589
Immediately after the loss Big E made them
play again.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:33.760
Sonic Fox’s Erron Black versus Perfect Legend’s
Kung Lao.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:40.800
And well, this one ended in similar fashion
to the games before it, a three round sweep,

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:44.520
and a 13-0 final score.

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:52.920
Coming in at number four, StarCraft 2 pros
HuK and IdrA.

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:55.370
The two players had a history.

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:59.820
HuK was an up-and-comer at the time, just
beginning his career in StarCraft 2.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:06.720
While IdrA was a veteran in the scene, starting
his career early in StarCraft: Brood War.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.520
There developed a hatred between the two.

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:13.360
"He's a piece of trash who wouldn't win any games if he didn't play Protoss."

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:21.510
The beef peaked at MLG Dallas 2011, where
their rivalry was perfectly summarized by

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:22.760
one play.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:25.720
"IdrA....left the game?"

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:35.200
HuK used hallucinated void rays to overwhelm
IdrA, who left the game thinking he'd lost.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:40.550
Of course, the hallucinations never dealt
any actual damage to IdrA.

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.830
HuK was sure to bring it up in the next game.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:49.510
The two would continue to battle until August
of 2011, when Evil Geniuses announced HuK

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:54.800
was joining their team, finally putting the rest this esports beef.

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:02.000
Leffen and Hungrybox’s relentless hate for
each other comes in at number three on this

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.480
list.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:11.800
Leffen and Hungrybox are both Super Smash
Bros. Melee players at the top of their esport.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:15.350
They also really hate each other.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:21.800
Hungrybox famously claims he only attended
Genesis 5 to kick Leffen’s ass.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:27.240
"It was the only reason I came to this tournament. I want to kick that guy's ass."

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:30.840
The two have beefed in-game and of course over Twitter

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:37.400
HBox also went on stream to gloat about beating
Leffen at Smash Summit 5.

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:44.320
"I three-oned his ass, three-oned him. Get f--ked. All your sh-t talk, get f--ked Leffen."

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.960
Leffen published a video to his YouTube, from
his stream, called “One of the Many Reasons

00:11:49.960 --> 00:11:51.960
to Dislike Hbox”.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:56.600
"The saying, there are two kind's of people in this world. HBox fans, and people who've met him."

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:04.680
One of the most infamous rivalries in the
FGC, KBrad and Wolfkrone do not hold back,

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:08.400
and that's why they are second on this list
of esports beefs.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:13.860
Their particular beef began in 2016, when
Wolfkrone wiped his hands of KBrad after beating

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:18.350
him in Street Fighter V at Frosty Faustings
9.

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:22.529
The beef escalated online, with tweets and
Facebook posts running rampant.

00:12:22.529 --> 00:12:29.360
KBrad got his revenge, and at the Capcom Pro
Tour, he slammed Wolfkrone in a 2-0 series.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:43.540
No respect has been shown in this beef and
the hatred between the two is high.

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:45.440
"Will this beef ever end or is this everlasting now?"

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:49.820
"I think there's something wrong with him. I legit think there's something wrong with his head."

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:53.960
The culmination of their beef thus far is
undoubtedly their run-in at

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.480
ELEAGUE’s Street Fighter V Invitational.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:01.880
"He's telling every excuse, 'I just can't deal with it, I just don't know how.' Every excuse is coming out the book now."

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:05.920
"As a player, I used to always fist-bump him I used to always shake his hand

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:07.920
until he started talking trash.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:10.600
But as a person, I just don't like him."

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:14.680
"Straight up, what are your thoughts on that man?"

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:15.360
"Who gives a f-ck."

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:21.760
"What did you do to change things here this tournament?"

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:27.240
"I mean I really didn't do anything, it's kinda felt a point for me

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:31.280
after we beat OpTic at Columbus. I don't want to say anything got boring but I was just kinda focusing on

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:32.200
other things."

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:40.720
OpTic gaming versus FaZe, a tale of two Call of Duty
giants, tops our list of esports beefs.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:45.110
The two teams would battle for online turf,
both producing similar YouTube content in

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:48.339
the early days of their respective orgs.

00:13:48.339 --> 00:13:53.769
OpTic would be the first to take on esports,
braving the rocky shores of the early scene.

00:13:53.769 --> 00:13:58.260
FaZe would eventually make their debut in
esports, bringing their large fanbase with

00:13:58.260 --> 00:13:59.260
them.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:04.230
Two giants, coming from the same place, now
had an arena to duke it out in.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:08.380
While OpTic had a huge head start, FaZe was
hot on their tail.

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:09.580
"FaZe takes UMG."

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:17.040
In the early days, OpTic and FaZe ran the
YouTube community online, and you either liked

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:18.840
OpTic or FaZe.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:20.120
Never both.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:27.800
While the Faze and Optic rivalry has calmed
down since, the impact of this esports beef

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:30.800
can still be felt today.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:40.510
Well guys if that wasn't some USDA Approved
Choice cut Prime beef, I don't know what is.

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:44.570
But if you think there is an esports beef
that should have been in our top ten list

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:45.570
and wasn't.

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:48.480
Well I got some bad news for you, you're just
wrong.

